<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrianjaka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 17th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrianjaka was a king of Imerina in the central highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> in the early seventeenth century, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralambo">Ralambo</a>. Tradition credits him with capturing the hill of Analamanga, which he renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a> and made the royal seat, and with acquiring firearms. He is remembered as one of the founders of the Merina kingdom.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Andrianjaka was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralambo">Ralambo</a>. He married Ravadifo, a daughter of Prince Andriampanarivomanjaka, and they had a daughter and a son, Andriantsitakatrandriana, who later succeeded him. Oral history has him helping to defend his father's capital at Ambohidrabiby against Antsihanaka warriors by filling its defensive trenches with smouldering cow dung and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> husks disguised as newly cleared land, into which the enemy marched.</p><p>Oral tradition tells his succession in two ways, both turning on a game of fanorona that kept one brother from answering his father's summons. Historians generally accept that the public rejected the claim of his elder brother Andriantompokoindrindra, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralambo">Ralambo</a> may have preferred Andrianjaka as the son of his first wife. In compensation, his reigning descendants were required to marry a princess descended from the elder brother.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On his accession, around 1610 or 1612, Andrianjaka moved his capital from Ambohidrabiby to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambohimanga">Ambohimanga</a>. He is said to have been the first Merina ruler to receive Europeans, around 1620, trading slaves for 50 guns and three barrels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>. He then seized Analamanga with a garrison of 1,000 soldiers, reportedly with little bloodshed, and his subjects were set to building dikes that turned the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/betsimitatatra">Betsimitatatra</a> below into surplus-producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> paddies.</p><p>He named twelve sacred hills of Imerina, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambohimanga">Ambohimanga</a>, Alasora, Ilafy and Analamanga, assigned each clan to a region of the kingdom, and restricted the sampy, the community talismans, to twelve whose making became a royal prerogative. He also changed the trial by ordeal so that the accused, rather than a rooster, swallowed the tangena poison.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Andrianjaka died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rova-of-antananarivo">Rova of Antananarivo</a> around 1630, the first king buried within it. His tomb was the first of the row called Fitomiandalana, the Seven Aligned Tombs, and his subjects raised over it a small wooden house, a trano masina, a custom followed on royal and noble tombs into the 19th century. The lengthy formal mourning of Merina sovereigns may also date from his death. The house Besakana, remembered as his residence, was rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriamasinavalona">Andriamasinavalona</a> around 1680 and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrianampoinimerina">Andrianampoinimerina</a> in 1800, and the buildings of the rova stood until a fire in 1995.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andrianjaka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Andrianjaka</b> is remembered as the king who first occupied the hill of Analamanga, renamed Antananarivo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/merina-kingdom">Merina Kingdom · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-18.91,47.53&amp;year=1664" aria-label="Antananarivo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5836 2864 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Andrianjaka"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6286 3134h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6286 3134h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-18.91,47.53&amp;year=1664">What was here in 1664? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
